      "title": "Passage 1: The Deep-Sea Coral Ecosystems of the North Atlantic",
      "text": "Paragraph A\nDeep-sea corals, also known as cold-water corals, thrive in ocean waters hundreds to thousands of meters beneath the surface, where sunlight cannot penetrate. Unlike their warm-water tropical relatives, which rely on photosynthetic zooxanthellae algae for energy, deep-sea corals exist in perpetual darkness and low temperatures ranging from 4 to 12 degrees Celsius. They derive all their nutritional requirements by capturing floating organic debris, micro-zooplankton, and marine snow carried by deep ocean currents. Recent oceanographic expeditions using remotely operated underwater vehicles (ROVs) have mapped extensive cold-water coral mounds along the continental margins of the North Atlantic, particularly off the coasts of Norway, Ireland, and Canada.\n\nParagraph B\nThe primary reef-building species in these cold marine environments is Lophelia pertusa, a stony coral that secretes a hard calcium carbonate skeleton. Over thousands of years, successive generations of Lophelia colonies grow atop older skeletal structures, gradually constructing complex three-dimensional reef mounds that can rise up to 30 meters high and stretch for several kilometers. These deep-sea structures serve as critical ecological hotspots in the otherwise vast, featureless abyssal plains. Marine biologists estimate that cold-water coral reefs support over 1,300 species of invertebrates and fish, providing shelter, feeding grounds, and spawning habitat for commercially valuable species such as redfish, cod, and ling.\n\nParagraph C\nDespite their remote location, deep-sea coral ecosystems face significant threats from human activities. Deep-sea bottom trawling, a commercial fishing practice involving heavy weighted nets dragged along the seafloor, has caused extensive physical destruction to fragile coral structures. Heavy steel trawl doors and nets break apart centuries-old coral colonies in seconds, converting intricate habitats into rubble. In response, international marine conservation organizations and coastal states have established Marine Protected Areas (MPAs) where bottom trawling is strictly prohibited. Furthermore, ocean acidification driven by anthropogenic carbon dioxide emissions threatens to reduce seawater carbonate ion concentrations, making it increasingly difficult for calcifying organisms like Lophelia pertusa to maintain their calcium carbonate skeletons.",
